# Purpose

flash installs one already-built target application on a physical board. It is
a separate operation from build and run: build produces an image, flash changes
device state, and run executes through a configured runner without programming
hardware. The initial implementation supports only the Raspberry Pi Pico SDK
platform family and uses picotool's UF2 loader.

# Command

Opening fence
./flash.sh [-v|--verbose] <app-manifest>
./flash [-v|--verbose] <app-manifest>
out/bin/flash [-v|--verbose] <app-manifest>
Closing fence

The manifest may be an app directory, its mm.mdy, or another spelling accepted
by the common manifest resolver. Exactly one registered kind: app is required.
Flashing always uses the retained configured target lane; there is no host mode
and the command does not change the persisted lane selection.

flash.sh requires out/bin/flash and does not rebuild it. When picotool_DIR is
unset, the root wrapper exports the local package installed at
platforms/pico/pico-sdk/picotool. An explicitly supplied picotool_DIR wins. The
no-extension flash wrapper follows the project's ordinary convention and routes
flash.sh through out/bin/shell when that tool exists.

# Preconditions

Before starting picotool, the tool requires all of the following:

- a retained target toolchain, target build directory, and configuration-2
  platform record;
- an SDK/board pair recognized by the Pico backend;
- a registered application available in that target lane;
- a regular UF2 supplement at <target-build-directory>/<app-dir>/<name>.uf2
  (for an external application, this is located under the external root's
  target build directory derived through the artifact context);
- picotool_DIR naming an existing directory containing a regular file named
  picotool.

The recognized base boards are pico, pico-w, pico2-arm, pico2-riscv,
pico2-w-arm, and pico2-w-riscv under pico-arm or pico-riscv as appropriate. A
derived board is also recognized when its resolved board chain contains a base
compatible with the selected SDK. This is the same distinction the Pico CMake
bridge makes: the project keeps the selected derived-board identity while the
base supplies Pico SDK's PICO_BOARD vocabulary. boards/pico_epaper therefore
uses the Pico backend through its pico base.

Project-linked rp2040-ram and rp2350-ram do not produce Pico SDK UF2 supplements
and are rejected. A board merely naming a Pico SDK is not sufficient; its
resolved chain must reach one of the recognized bases. Other platforms remain
future backends rather than aliases for the Pico behavior.

Configuration staleness, structural properties, platform-provider resolution,
and target availability are checked with the same shared machinery as build,
test, run, and debug. A missing image says to run build first. flash never starts
a build implicitly, so the file installed is always the file the user can inspect
before changing a device.

# Picotool invocation

The current backend executes exactly:

Opening fence
picotool load -v -x <application.uf2>
Closing fence

- load programs the UF2 image;
- -v verifies the written data;
- -x asks the boot ROM to reboot and execute the new image after loading.

The picotool executable and image path are individually shell-quoted through
mm.build before the common process wrapper executes them. Picotool's exit status
is returned to the caller. The command deliberately omits -f: a board running an
application without compatible USB support cannot be forced into BOOTSEL through
picotool. The reliable first-use procedure is to hold BOOTSEL while connecting
USB, release it after the device appears, and then invoke flash.

# Example

For a plain Raspberry Pi Pico:

Opening fence
./configure --target arm-none-eabi --compiler arm-none-eabi-gcc \
  --sdk pico-arm --board pico --build debug
picotool_DIR="$PWD/platforms/pico/pico-sdk/picotool" \
  ./build --target apps/mcu-blink/
./flash.sh -v apps/mcu-blink/
Closing fence

The build step publishes both the extensionless ELF and
out-target-arm-none-eabi/apps/mcu-blink/mcu-blink.uf2. flash selects the UF2; it
never sends the extensionless ELF by accident.

# Conformance

- mm.flash unit tests cover the six base boards, derived-board ancestry,
  incompatible and unknown chains, rejection of a project-linked RP2040
  platform, UF2 path derivation, safe quoting, and child status propagation
  without accessing hardware.
- tools/flash validates project membership, target availability, configuration
  freshness, the image, and picotool before executing an external command.
- flash.sh reports a missing installed tool and supplies only the repository's
  local picotool package default.
- No automated test flashes a device. Actual programming remains an explicit,
  user-initiated operation.

# Current boundaries

- Only picotool and Pico SDK UF2 images are supported.
- Device selection options are not yet forwarded. With multiple boards in
  BOOTSEL, picotool decides whether its default selection is unambiguous.
- Flash success proves that picotool programmed and verified the image. It does
  not prove application-level behavior after the reboot.
